 ## Context-bounded territories and complete PRD scopes
 
-Treat context fit as a constraint on PRD authoring, review, and implementation, not only dispatch. Bound what each person or agent must consider together while preserving the complete design, exact contracts, useful rationale, and evidence. A complete packet may span linked files and several bounded sessions; it must not require every recipient to retain the entire corpus at once.
-
-A **territory** is a bounded area of design responsibility with owned decisions, state or rules, allocated obligations, dependencies, and integration duties. Carry forward upstream territories using existing identities where possible. They are not automatically repositories, services, deployment units, PRDs, agents, or delivery phases. One PRD may sit within a territory or span several when it forms a coherent contribution. Keep small work as one scope. Do not change runtime boundaries merely to shorten a document; tightly coupled design may need separate review questions rather than separate owners.
-
-Give each substantive authoring or review task and each implementation slice a concise entry point: outcome and limits; owned and inherited obligations; fixed decisions and permitted choices; exact source sections, contract revisions, and evidence; incoming and outgoing dependencies; integration and acceptance owners; open items; and first substantive work. Distinguish material to read now, retrieve for a specific check, and retain as background. These reading roles are separate from the reference-availability classes in section 11. Summaries may orient a reader but cannot replace needed controlling definitions, exceptions, or evidence.
-
